    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, '101: Which anthropometric measurement is most sensitive for assessing acute malnutrition (wasting)?', '["Height-for-age", "Weight-for-length (or BMI-for-age)", "Head circumference-for-age", "Mid-upper arm circumference (MUAC)"]', 1, 'Weight-for-length (or BMI) drops rapidly in acute starvation. MUAC is also an excellent tool used in field settings for SAM (Severe Acute Malnutrition).'),
    (v_topic_id, '102: A child with a weight-for-length z-score of less than -3 represents:', '["Mild malnutrition", "Moderate malnutrition", "Severe acute malnutrition (SAM)", "Normal variant"]', 2, 'A z-score <-3 (or <70% of median) defines severe acute malnutrition.'),
    (v_topic_id, '103: Stunting (chronic malnutrition) is best defined by which parameter?', '["Weight-for-length < 5th percentile", "Height-for-age < 5th percentile (or z-score < -2)", "Head circumference < 5th percentile", "BMI > 95th percentile"]', 1, 'Stunting reflects long-term, chronic nutritional deficits that permanently affect linear growth.'),
    (v_topic_id, '104: A 4-month-old infant is exclusively breastfed. The mother is a strict vegan and refuses all supplements. The infant is most at risk for developing which type of anemia?', '["Microcytic hypochromic", "Macrocytic megaloblastic", "Normocytic normochromic", "Hemolytic"]', 1, 'Vitamin B12 deficiency (found only in animal products) causes a macrocytic megaloblastic anemia.'),
    (v_topic_id, '105: Which hormone is primarily responsible for promoting appetite (the "hunger hormone")?', '["Leptin", "Insulin", "Ghrelin", "Peptide YY"]', 2, 'Ghrelin is secreted by the stomach and stimulates appetite, while leptin (from fat cells) suppresses it.'),
    (v_topic_id, '106: A child with Prader-Willi syndrome has hyperphagia. This is often associated with dysregulation of which hormone?', '["Insulin", "Glucagon", "Ghrelin", "Cortisol"]', 2, 'Patients with PWS often have markedly elevated ghrelin levels, driving their insatiable appetite.'),
    (v_topic_id, '107: A 2-year-old child from a low-income family is observed eating paint chips from an old window sill. What nutritional deficiency must be screened for?', '["Vitamin C", "Calcium", "Iron", "Vitamin B12"]', 2, 'Pica is strongly associated with iron deficiency, and eating old paint chips puts the child at extreme risk for lead poisoning.'),
    (v_topic_id, '108: Lead toxicity in a child can cause which classic hematologic finding on peripheral smear?', '["Howell-Jolly bodies", "Basophilic stippling", "Heinz bodies", "Schistocytes"]', 1, 'Lead inhibits rRNA degradation, leaving clumps of RNA in RBCs visible as basophilic stippling.'),
    (v_topic_id, '109: What is the medical treatment for a child with a lead level >45 mcg/dL?', '["Iron supplementation only", "Observation", "Chelation therapy (e.g., Succimer or Dimercaprol/EDTA)", "Exchange transfusion"]', 2, 'Chelation is indicated to rapidly remove lead and prevent encephalopathy.'),
    (v_topic_id, '110: A 14-year-old female presents with fatigue and heavy menses. Hgb is 9.0 g/dL, MCV is 72 fL. Which iron preparation is standard first-line therapy?', '["Intravenous iron sucrose", "Oral ferrous sulfate (or gluconate)", "Intramuscular iron dextran", "Blood transfusion"]', 1, 'Oral iron is the first-line treatment for iron deficiency anemia unless there is severe malabsorption or life-threatening anemia.'),
    (v_topic_id, '111: When prescribing oral iron for a toddler, what dietary advice improves absorption?', '["Take with a glass of milk", "Take with Vitamin C (e.g., orange juice)", "Take with antacids", "Take with black tea"]', 1, 'Ascorbic acid keeps iron in the reduced (Fe2+) state, which is more readily absorbed in the duodenum.'),
    (v_topic_id, '112: Which substance in black tea strongly inhibits the absorption of non-heme iron?', '["Caffeine", "Tannins", "Oxalates", "Phytates"]', 1, 'Tannins bind to iron in the gut, forming insoluble complexes.'),
    (v_topic_id, '113: A mother gives her 6-month-old infant cow''s milk instead of formula because it is cheaper. Besides iron deficiency, what other immediate risk does this pose?', '["Hypercalcemia", "High renal solute load leading to dehydration", "Vitamin C toxicity", "Excessive fat intake"]', 1, 'Cow''s milk has a very high protein and mineral content (renal solute load) that a young infant''s kidneys cannot handle efficiently, risking severe hypernatremic dehydration.'),
    (v_topic_id, '114: A child with severe cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency presents with a prolonged prothrombin time (PT). Which vitamin is deficient?', '["Vitamin A", "Vitamin D", "Vitamin E", "Vitamin K"]', 3, 'Vitamin K is fat-soluble and required for factors II, VII, IX, and X. Deficiency prolongs the PT.'),
    (v_topic_id, '115: A child on long-term broad-spectrum antibiotics develops easy bruising and epistaxis. Which vitamin deficiency is likely contributing?', '["Vitamin C", "Vitamin K", "Vitamin B12", "Folate"]', 1, 'Normal gut flora synthesize a significant portion of Vitamin K (menaquinone). Antibiotics wipe out these bacteria.'),
    (v_topic_id, '116: An infant presents with bulging fontanelle, vomiting, and irritability after the parents accidentally gave highly concentrated formula (mixed with too little water). What electrolyte abnormality is expected?', '["Hyponatremia", "Hypernatremia", "Hypokalemia", "Hypercalcemia"]', 1, 'Concentrated formula provides a massive sodium/solute load, pulling free water out of cells and causing hypernatremic dehydration.'),
    (v_topic_id, '117: How should hypernatremic dehydration be corrected?', '["Rapidly with normal saline boluses", "Slowly over 48 hours to prevent cerebral edema", "Rapidly with 5% Dextrose in water", "With hypertonic saline"]', 1, 'Rapid correction of hypernatremia causes fluid to rush into brain cells (which generated idiogenic osmoles), leading to lethal cerebral edema.'),
    (v_topic_id, '118: A mother brings her 3-month-old infant to the ER with seizures. She admits to diluting the infant formula with extra water to make it last longer. What is the cause of the seizures?', '["Hypernatremia", "Hyponatremia", "Hypocalcemia", "Hypoglycemia"]', 1, 'Excess free water dilutes serum sodium. Water shifts into brain cells, causing cerebral edema and hyponatremic seizures.'),
    (v_topic_id, '119: An exclusively breastfed premature infant is at high risk for "Osteopenia of Prematurity." What two minerals must be supplemented?', '["Iron and Zinc", "Calcium and Phosphorus", "Sodium and Potassium", "Magnesium and Copper"]', 1, 'The majority of fetal calcium and phosphorus accretion happens in the 3rd trimester. Preemies miss this and breast milk does not contain enough to support catch-up bone growth.'),
    (v_topic_id, '120: Which vitamin enhances the intestinal absorption of Calcium and Phosphorus?', '["Vitamin A", "Vitamin C", "Vitamin D", "Vitamin K"]', 2, '1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (active Vitamin D) upregulates calcium binding proteins in the enterocyte.'),
    (v_topic_id, '121: A child with chronic renal failure develops "renal osteodystrophy." Why does this occur?', '["The kidneys excrete too much calcium", "The kidneys cannot convert 25-OH Vitamin D to the active 1,25-OH Vitamin D", "The kidneys excrete too much phosphorus", "The kidneys overproduce Vitamin D"]', 1, 'The 1-alpha-hydroxylase enzyme is located in the proximal tubule. Failure leads to active Vitamin D deficiency and secondary hyperparathyroidism.'),
    (v_topic_id, '122: In a child with Vitamin D deficiency rickets, what are the typical levels of Calcium, Phosphorus, and Parathyroid Hormone (PTH)?', '["Low Ca, Low Phos, Low PTH", "Normal/Low Ca, Low Phos, High PTH", "High Ca, High Phos, Low PTH", "Low Ca, High Phos, High PTH"]', 1, 'Lack of Vitamin D lowers Ca absorption. This triggers PTH release (secondary hyperparathyroidism). PTH normalizes Ca (often keeping it low-normal) but dumps Phosphorus in the urine, leading to low Phos.'),
    (v_topic_id, '123: What is the most sensitive lab marker for confirming a diagnosis of active Vitamin D deficiency rickets?', '["Alkaline phosphatase (elevated)", "Serum calcium (decreased)", "Serum phosphorus (elevated)", "Urine calcium (elevated)"]', 0, 'Alkaline phosphatase is markedly elevated due to massive osteoblast activity trying to mineralize the defective bone.'),
    (v_topic_id, '124: Which nutritional deficiency is associated with "spoon-shaped nails" (koilonychia)?', '["Vitamin C", "Iron", "Zinc", "Protein"]', 1, 'Koilonychia is a classic sign of chronic, severe iron deficiency.'),
    (v_topic_id, '125: A 16-year-old female runner presents with a stress fracture, amenorrhea for 6 months, and a BMI of 16. What is the diagnosis?', '["Polycystic ovary syndrome", "Female Athlete Triad", "Turner syndrome", "Hypothyroidism"]', 1, 'The triad consists of low energy availability (with or without eating disorder), menstrual dysfunction, and low bone mineral density.'),
    (v_topic_id, '126: What is the primary treatment for the Female Athlete Triad?', '["Estrogen replacement therapy", "Bisphosphonates", "Increasing caloric intake and reducing energy expenditure", "Calcium supplements only"]', 2, 'Restoring energy balance is the only way to reverse the hypothalamic amenorrhea and prevent irreversible bone loss.'),
    (v_topic_id, '127: A 2-year-old child has a very restricted diet, eating only white bread and milk. He has no body image distortion, just a severe aversion to the texture of other foods. He is falling off the growth curve. Diagnosis?', '["Anorexia nervosa", "Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder (ARFID)", "Bulimia nervosa", "Autism spectrum disorder"]', 1, 'While common in autism, ARFID itself is the diagnosis for clinically significant, restrictive eating driven by sensory aversions without weight-concern.'),
    (v_topic_id, '128: A child is admitted with severe marasmus. During the first few days of refeeding, what is the most critical monitoring parameter?', '["Serum albumin", "Serum electrolytes (especially Potassium, Phosphorus, Magnesium)", "Hemoglobin", "Liver enzymes"]', 1, 'Monitoring for Refeeding Syndrome (hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia) is life-saving.'),
    (v_topic_id, '129: What happens to serum phosphorus during Refeeding Syndrome?', '["It rises due to tissue breakdown", "It drops rapidly as insulin drives it into cells for ATP synthesis", "It remains unchanged", "It is excreted in the urine"]', 1, 'The sudden shift from fat to carbohydrate metabolism causes a massive insulin spike, which drives K, Mg, and PO4 into cells, severely depleting serum levels.'),
    (v_topic_id, '130: Which clinical manifestation is most likely to kill a patient experiencing severe Refeeding Syndrome?', '["Hepatic failure", "Cardiac arrhythmias and heart failure", "Renal failure", "Seizures"]', 1, 'Profound hypophosphatemia and hypokalemia lead to ATP depletion in the myocardium, causing arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death.'),
    (v_topic_id, '131: A neonate with short bowel syndrome requires long-term lipid emulsions in their TPN. Which essential fatty acids must be provided to prevent deficiency?', '["Stearic and Palmitic acid", "Linoleic and Alpha-linolenic acid", "Oleic acid", "Cholesterol"]', 1, 'Omega-6 (linoleic) and Omega-3 (alpha-linolenic) are essential. Deficiency causes scaly dermatitis, alopecia, and poor wound healing.'),
    (v_topic_id, '132: A 6-month-old infant presents with a generalized seizure. The infant is exclusively fed a homemade formula made from soy milk and honey. Calcium is 6.0 mg/dL. What caused the hypocalcemia?', '["Inadequate Vitamin D and Calcium in the homemade formula", "Botulism from the honey", "Soy allergy", "Hyperparathyroidism"]', 0, 'Homemade formulas are extremely dangerous and lack essential nutrients like calcium and vitamin D, leading to hypocalcemic tetany/seizures.'),
    (v_topic_id, '133: A child presents with "Chvostek sign" (facial muscle twitching when the facial nerve is tapped). What is the underlying electrolyte abnormality?', '["Hypercalcemia", "Hypocalcemia", "Hyperkalemia", "Hyponatremia"]', 1, 'Chvostek and Trousseau signs indicate neuromuscular irritability due to hypocalcemia.'),
    (v_topic_id, '134: A child with a history of extensive bowel resection develops night blindness. Which fat-soluble vitamin is deficient?', '["A", "D", "E", "K"]', 0, 'Vitamin A deficiency (xerophthalmia) is a classic complication of fat malabsorption.'),
    (v_topic_id, '135: A child with chronic cholestasis (liver disease) develops spinocerebellar ataxia, absent deep tendon reflexes, and proprioceptive loss. Which vitamin deficiency is causing this neurologic picture?', '["Vitamin A", "Vitamin B12", "Vitamin E", "Vitamin K"]', 2, 'Vitamin E deficiency classically causes a neurologic syndrome mimicking Friedreich ataxia or B12 deficiency (ataxia, hyporeflexia, loss of position/vibration sense).'),
    (v_topic_id, '136: A 3-year-old child presents with bowed legs. They have a history of a rare genetic defect in the renal proximal tubule leading to massive phosphorus wasting. Diagnosis?', '["Nutritional rickets", "X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets (Vitamin D resistant rickets)", "Osteogenesis imperfecta", "Scurvy"]', 1, 'This condition does not respond to standard Vitamin D. It requires oral phosphorus and active Vitamin D (calcitriol) supplementation.'),
    (v_topic_id, '137: Which hormone is excessively produced in X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets, causing the renal phosphate wasting?', '["PTH", "FGF23", "Calcitonin", "Aldosterone"]', 1, 'Fibroblast Growth Factor 23 (FGF23) inhibits phosphate reabsorption in the kidney.'),
    (v_topic_id, '138: A teenager presents with fatigue, cold intolerance, constipation, and weight gain. They have a firm, enlarged thyroid gland. Diet is normal. Diagnosis?', '["Iodine deficiency", "Hashimoto thyroiditis", "Graves disease", "Anorexia nervosa"]', 1, 'While iodine deficiency is the most common cause worldwide, Hashimoto''s is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient areas (like the US).'),
    (v_topic_id, '139: A child on a strict vegan diet for 3 years without supplements presents with a smooth, red, "beefy" tongue (glossitis) and tingling in the feet. Deficient nutrient?', '["Vitamin B12", "Iron", "Vitamin C", "Vitamin A"]', 0, 'Glossitis and peripheral neuropathy are classic for B12 deficiency.'),
    (v_topic_id, '140: How does folate deficiency differ clinically from Vitamin B12 deficiency?', '["Folate deficiency causes microcytic anemia", "Folate deficiency does not cause neurologic symptoms", "Folate deficiency causes jaundice", "Folate deficiency is found in animal products"]', 1, 'Both cause megaloblastic anemia and glossitis, but only B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord (neurologic symptoms).'),
    (v_topic_id, '141: A child presents with severe gingivitis, bleeding gums, and petechiae. They eat only macaroni and cheese. Deficient nutrient?', '["Vitamin C", "Vitamin K", "Iron", "Zinc"]', 0, 'Classic presentation of Scurvy.'),
    (v_topic_id, '142: Vitamin C is a necessary cofactor for the hydroxylation of which amino acids in collagen synthesis?', '["Phenylalanine and Tyrosine", "Proline and Lysine", "Glycine and Alanine", "Methionine and Cysteine"]', 1, 'Without hydroxylated proline/lysine, collagen cannot form its stable triple helix structure.'),
    (v_topic_id, '143: A child has a genetic defect in the enzyme glucose-6-phosphatase (Von Gierke disease). They are at extreme risk for which metabolic derangement during brief periods of fasting?', '["Hyperglycemia", "Severe, life-threatening hypoglycemia", "Hypocalcemia", "Hyperkalemia"]', 1, 'They cannot release glucose from the liver via glycogenolysis or gluconeogenesis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '144: To prevent hypoglycemia, children with Von Gierke disease are often treated with continuous nighttime feeds of what substance?', '["Uncooked cornstarch", "Fructose", "Galactose", "Protein shakes"]', 0, 'Uncooked cornstarch provides a slow, steady release of glucose in the gut.'),
    (v_topic_id, '145: A neonate presents with cataracts, hepatomegaly, and failure to thrive after starting milk feeds. Urine is positive for reducing substances, but negative for glucose. Diagnosis?', '["Galactosemia", "Hereditary Fructose Intolerance", "Diabetes mellitus", "PKU"]', 0, 'Galactose acts as a reducing substance in the urine. Glucose is negative on specific dipstick.'),
    (v_topic_id, '146: What is the deficient enzyme in classic Galactosemia?', '["Galactokinase", "Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (GALT)", "Aldolase B", "Fructokinase"]', 1, 'GALT deficiency causes accumulation of toxic Galactose-1-phosphate in the liver and brain.'),
    (v_topic_id, '147: A child is healthy while breastfeeding, but develops severe hypoglycemia, vomiting, and jaundice when fruit juices and purees are introduced at 6 months. Diagnosis?', '["Galactosemia", "Hereditary Fructose Intolerance", "Celiac disease", "Type 1 Diabetes"]', 1, 'Aldolase B deficiency causes fructose-1-phosphate accumulation. Symptoms start when fructose/sucrose is introduced.'),
    (v_topic_id, '148: A child with cystic fibrosis is admitted with a severe pulmonary exacerbation. What adjustment must be made to their pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy (PERT)?', '["Stop the enzymes", "Decrease the dose", "Increase the dose to match increased caloric intake and fat requirements", "Switch to intravenous enzymes"]', 2, 'During illness, caloric needs increase, and fat intake must be maximized. PERT must be titrated to dietary fat intake to prevent malabsorption.'),
    (v_topic_id, '149: A 2-year-old child presents with a distended abdomen, flat buttocks (muscle wasting), and chronic foul-smelling diarrhea. The parents note the child is very irritable. IgA tissue transglutaminase antibodies are positive. What is the gold standard for diagnosis?', '["Stool culture", "Small bowel biopsy showing villous atrophy", "Sweat chloride test", "Genetic testing for HLA-DQ2/DQ8"]', 1, 'Biopsy of the duodenum showing villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis confirms Celiac disease.'),
    (v_topic_id, '150: A child with Down Syndrome has a high risk of developing which malabsorptive disorder?', '["Cystic Fibrosis", "Celiac Disease", "Crohn''s Disease", "Ulcerative Colitis"]', 1, 'Children with Trisomy 21 have a 5-15% risk of Celiac disease and should be screened.'),
    (v_topic_id, '151: A 15-year-old girl with a BMI of 14 is admitted for severe anorexia nervosa. On day 3 of refeeding, she develops generalized edema. What is the cause?', '["Heart failure from hypophosphatemia", "Insulin-mediated sodium and water retention", "Renal failure", "Liver failure"]', 1, 'Refeeding edema is common and results from the sudden presence of insulin, which promotes renal sodium reabsorption.'),
    (v_topic_id, '152: A patient with Anorexia Nervosa has secondary amenorrhea. What is the primary hormonal mechanism?', '["Ovarian failure", "Suppression of hypothalamic GnRH pulsatility", "Hyperprolactinemia", "Thyroid failure"]', 1, 'Starvation and low leptin levels suppress the HPG axis to conserve energy, leading to functional hypothalamic amenorrhea.'),
    (v_topic_id, '153: A patient with Bulimia Nervosa frequently uses ipecac syrup to induce vomiting. They are at risk for which life-threatening complication?', '["Cardiomyopathy (myotoxicity from emetine)", "Renal failure", "Liver failure", "Pulmonary fibrosis"]', 0, 'Ipecac contains emetine, which is directly toxic to the myocardium with repeated use.'),
    (v_topic_id, '154: Which physical exam finding is classically seen in patients with Bulimia Nervosa due to frequent vomiting?', '["Parotid gland hypertrophy", "Thyroid enlargement", "Lymphadenopathy", "Splenomegaly"]', 0, 'Repeated vomiting causes non-inflammatory enlargement of the salivary glands ("chipmunk cheeks").'),
    (v_topic_id, '155: A child has a severe allergy to cow''s milk. Which of the following ingredients on a food label indicates the presence of milk protein?', '["Casein", "Whey", "Lactalbumin", "All of the above"]', 3, 'All these terms refer to specific milk proteins and must be avoided.'),
    (v_topic_id, '156: A child presents with chronic diarrhea and poor weight gain. Stool analysis shows an acidic pH (<5.5) and positive reducing substances. Diagnosis?', '["Protein-losing enteropathy", "Carbohydrate malabsorption (e.g., lactose intolerance)", "Fat malabsorption", "Secretory diarrhea"]', 1, 'Unabsorbed carbohydrates are fermented by colonic bacteria into short-chain fatty acids (lowering pH) and act as reducing substances.'),
    (v_topic_id, '157: What is the most common cause of acquired lactose intolerance in a toddler?', '["Genetic mutation", "Post-infectious gastroenteritis (transient lactase deficiency)", "Celiac disease", "Cystic fibrosis"]', 1, 'Viral gastroenteritis (e.g., Rotavirus) strips the tips of the intestinal villi where lactase resides. It takes weeks to regenerate.'),
    (v_topic_id, '158: A child with short bowel syndrome requires long-term elemental formula. What characterizes an elemental formula?', '["Intact proteins and long-chain fats", "Amino acids, simple sugars, and medium-chain triglycerides (MCTs)", "High lactose content", "High fiber"]', 1, 'Elemental formulas require virtually no digestion; MCTs are absorbed directly into the portal vein without needing bile salts or lymphatics.'),
    (v_topic_id, '159: A newborn has a severe defect in the urea cycle (e.g., Ornithine Transcarbamylase deficiency). Which macronutrient must be strictly limited to prevent lethal hyperammonemia?', '["Carbohydrates", "Fats", "Protein", "Water"]', 2, 'The urea cycle disposes of ammonia generated from protein breakdown. Excess protein causes neurotoxic ammonia accumulation.'),
    (v_topic_id, '160: A neonate presents with lethargy, vomiting, and tachypnea. Labs show severe hyperammonemia, respiratory alkalosis, and undetectable citrulline. Diagnosis?', '["PKU", "Ornithine Transcarbamylase (OTC) Deficiency", "Galactosemia", "Cystic fibrosis"]', 1, 'OTC is an X-linked recessive urea cycle defect.'),
    (v_topic_id, '161: A child with PKU must avoid artificial sweeteners containing which substance?', '["Sucralose", "Saccharin", "Aspartame", "Stevia"]', 2, 'Aspartame is metabolized into aspartic acid and phenylalanine.'),
    (v_topic_id, '162: Maternal PKU (a mother with PKU who does not maintain a strict diet during pregnancy) causes what congenital defects in the fetus?', '["Macrosomia and hypoglycemia", "Microcephaly, intellectual disability, and congenital heart defects", "Limb reduction defects", "Neural tube defects"]', 1, 'High maternal phenylalanine levels are highly teratogenic to the developing fetus.'),
    (v_topic_id, '163: A 4-year-old child from a developing country presents with a prominent abdomen, generalized edema, and alternating bands of light and dark hair ("flag sign"). Diagnosis?', '["Marasmus", "Kwashiorkor", "Iron deficiency", "Vitamin C deficiency"]', 1, 'The "flag sign" represents periods of poor protein intake (light hair) alternating with adequate intake (dark hair).'),
    (v_topic_id, '164: In a child with severe protein-energy malnutrition (Kwashiorkor), why is intravenous albumin administration generally avoided despite low serum albumin?', '["It causes anaphylaxis", "It rapidly increases intravascular volume, leading to acute heart failure in a weakened heart", "It causes renal failure", "It causes hypoglycemia"]', 1, 'The atrophied myocardium cannot handle the sudden fluid shift caused by oncotic pressure from IV albumin.'),
    (v_topic_id, '165: A child has a rare autosomal recessive defect causing impaired intestinal absorption of neutral amino acids, including tryptophan (Hartnup disease). They are at risk for developing symptoms of which vitamin deficiency?', '["Vitamin B1", "Vitamin B3 (Niacin)", "Vitamin C", "Vitamin D"]', 1, 'Tryptophan is a precursor to niacin. Deficiency leads to pellagra-like symptoms (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia).'),
    (v_topic_id, '166: A child presents with recurrent severe infections and a generalized scaly, erythematous rash. They lack the enzyme necessary to absorb Zinc. Diagnosis?', '["Acrodermatitis enteropathica", "Menkes disease", "Cystic fibrosis", "Celiac disease"]', 0, 'This is a rare autosomal recessive disorder of zinc absorption, treatable with lifelong high-dose oral zinc.'),
    (v_topic_id, '167: Which of the following diets is considered a first-line medical therapy for children with intractable, drug-resistant epilepsy?', '["Gluten-free diet", "Ketogenic diet", "Low-protein diet", "High-carbohydrate diet"]', 1, 'The ketogenic diet (high fat, very low carb) forces the brain to use ketones, which has a strong anticonvulsant effect.'),
    (v_topic_id, '168: A child on a strict ketogenic diet is at increased risk for developing which of the following complications?', '["Kidney stones (nephrolithiasis)", "Type 1 Diabetes", "Celiac disease", "Asthma"]', 0, 'The acidic nature of the diet and altered calcium metabolism predispose to uric acid and calcium kidney stones.'),
    (v_topic_id, '169: A breastfed infant presents with severe perianal dermatitis and diarrhea. The mother recently took a course of antibiotics. The rash is fiery red with "satellite lesions." Diagnosis?', '["Zinc deficiency", "Candidal diaper dermatitis", "Cow''s milk allergy", "Acrodermatitis enteropathica"]', 1, 'Antibiotics alter gut flora, allowing Candida overgrowth. Satellite lesions are classic.'),
    (v_topic_id, '170: A mother asks when she can introduce honey to her infant to sweeten oatmeal. What is the correct advice?', '["At 6 months", "After 12 months", "Only if it is pasteurized", "Never"]', 1, 'Honey can contain Clostridium botulinum spores. Infants lack the gut acidity/flora to prevent colonization, leading to infant botulism (floppy baby syndrome).'),
    (v_topic_id, '171: At what age is it developmentally appropriate to begin transitioning an infant from a bottle to a sippy cup/open cup?', '["4 months", "6-9 months", "18 months", "2 years"]', 1, 'Early introduction helps develop oral-motor skills and prevents prolonged bottle use.'),
    (v_topic_id, '172: What is the primary concern with allowing a toddler to sleep with a bottle of milk or juice?', '["Aspiration", "Early childhood caries (baby bottle tooth decay)", "Hypernatremia", "Diarrhea"]', 1, 'Pooling of sugar-rich liquids around the teeth overnight promotes severe, rapid decay of the maxillary incisors.'),
    (v_topic_id, '173: A 3-year-old child weighs 14 kg. What is their daily maintenance fluid requirement?', '["1000 mL/day", "1200 mL/day", "1400 mL/day", "1600 mL/day"]', 1, 'Using the 100-50-20 rule: 100mL for the first 10kg (1000mL) + 50mL for the next 4kg (200mL) = 1200 mL/day.'),
    (v_topic_id, '174: A 25-kg child is admitted for dehydration. What is their maintenance IV fluid rate per hour?', '["45 mL/hr", "65 mL/hr", "85 mL/hr", "100 mL/hr"]', 1, 'Using the 4-2-1 rule: 40mL (first 10kg) + 20mL (next 10kg) + 5mL (last 5kg) = 65 mL/hr.'),
    (v_topic_id, '175: In a child with moderate dehydration (6-9%), what is the preferred initial method of rehydration?', '["Intravenous Normal Saline bolus", "Oral Rehydration Therapy (ORT) with an appropriate electrolyte solution", "Intravenous 5% Dextrose", "Plain water"]', 1, 'ORT is as effective as IV fluids for mild to moderate dehydration and has fewer complications.'),
    (v_topic_id, '176: Oral Rehydration Solutions (ORS) rely on which physiological mechanism in the intestine to absorb water?', '["Passive diffusion of water", "The sodium-glucose cotransporter (SGLT1)", "Active transport of chloride", "Potassium channels"]', 1, 'Sodium and glucose are co-transported into the enterocyte, creating an osmotic gradient that pulls water with them.'),
    (v_topic_id, '177: A child with severe dehydration (10% or greater) presents in hypovolemic shock. What is the immediate first step?', '["Oral rehydration", "20 mL/kg bolus of isotonic crystalloid (Normal Saline or Ringer''s Lactate)", "10 mL/kg bolus of hypertonic saline", "Dextrose infusion"]', 1, 'Intravenous volume expansion is life-saving in severe dehydration with compromised perfusion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '178: An infant presents with vomiting and diarrhea. Capillary refill is 4 seconds, eyes are sunken, and the infant is lethargic. What degree of dehydration is this?', '["Mild (3-5%)", "Moderate (6-9%)", "Severe (>=10%)", "None"]', 2, 'Lethargy, delayed capillary refill (>3 sec), and sunken eyes indicate severe dehydration.'),
    (v_topic_id, '179: A neonate born at 32 weeks gestation requires TPN. Why must lipids be infused slowly?', '["To prevent hypercholesterolemia", "To avoid overwhelming the reticuloendothelial system and worsening lung disease/pulmonary hypertension", "To prevent hypoglycemia", "To prevent gallstones"]', 1, 'Rapid lipid infusion can impair macrophage function and worsen pulmonary gas exchange in preemies.'),
    (v_topic_id, '180: A 14-year-old female presents with primary amenorrhea, lack of breast development, and short stature. What is the most likely cause?', '["Anorexia nervosa", "Turner syndrome (45,X)", "Hypothyroidism", "Constitutional growth delay"]', 1, 'While anorexia causes secondary amenorrhea, primary amenorrhea with short stature points strongly to Turner syndrome.'),
    (v_topic_id, '181: A child with obesity presents with bilateral bowing of the legs (genu varum). X-rays show a step-off at the medial proximal tibial physis. Diagnosis?', '["Rickets", "Blount disease (Tibia vara)", "Osgood-Schlatter disease", "Normal variant"]', 1, 'Blount disease is an acquired growth disorder of the medial tibia exacerbated by obesity and early walking.'),
    (v_topic_id, '182: What is the most effective intervention for a 10-year-old child with a BMI > 95th percentile?', '["Bariatric surgery", "Strict calorie restriction (e.g., 800 kcal/day)", "Family-based behavioral lifestyle intervention (diet and exercise)", "Pharmacotherapy (e.g., Orlistat)"]', 2, 'Family-based interventions addressing the entire household''s diet and activity are the gold standard for pediatric obesity.'),
    (v_topic_id, '183: A 15-year-old boy has a BMI of 45 (severe obesity) and type 2 diabetes poorly controlled on insulin. He has sleep apnea. What intervention has the best long-term success?', '["Metformin", "Bariatric surgery (e.g., sleeve gastrectomy)", "Dietary counseling", "Thyroid hormone"]', 1, 'Adolescents with severe obesity (BMI > 40 or > 35 with severe comorbidities) who fail lifestyle changes are candidates for bariatric surgery.'),
    (v_topic_id, '184: A child with cystic fibrosis presents with a prolapsed rectum. This is a classic complication of:', '["Severe coughing", "Constipation", "Bulky, malabsorbed stools due to pancreatic insufficiency", "Portal hypertension"]', 2, 'The frequent, large, greasy stools put stress on the pelvic floor. It often resolves with proper enzyme replacement.'),
    (v_topic_id, '185: A 1-month-old infant presents with prolonged jaundice, an umbilical hernia, and a large anterior fontanelle. They are hypotonic and feed poorly. Diagnosis?', '["Congenital Hypothyroidism", "Down Syndrome", "Sepsis", "Galactosemia"]', 0, 'This is a classic presentation of congenital hypothyroidism ("cretinism"). Early screening and treatment prevent intellectual disability.'),
    (v_topic_id, '186: A 2-year-old immigrant child presents with severe bowing of the legs, a prominent forehead (frontal bossing), and a waddling gait. Diagnosis?', '["Scurvy", "Rickets (Vitamin D deficiency)", "Osteogenesis imperfecta", "Child abuse"]', 1, 'These are classic skeletal deformities seen in advanced rickets once the child starts weight-bearing.'),
    (v_topic_id, '187: Which of the following is an early radiologic sign of Scurvy (Vitamin C deficiency)?', '["Cupping of metaphyses", "Ground-glass osteopenia and a white line of Frankel at the metaphysis", "Multiple fractures in different stages of healing", "Bone tumors"]', 1, 'The dense zone of provisional calcification (Frankel line) occurs because osteoid is not formed, but calcium still deposits.'),
    (v_topic_id, '188: A mother brings her 4-month-old infant in for a checkup. She makes all her own baby food by blending fresh vegetables, including spinach, beets, and carrots. What rare but serious condition is the infant at risk for?', '["Methemoglobinemia", "Vitamin A toxicity", "Iron overload", "Scurvy"]', 0, 'Root vegetables and spinach are high in nitrates. Infants under 6 months lack the stomach acid to prevent bacteria from converting nitrates to nitrites, which oxidize hemoglobin to methemoglobin.'),
    (v_topic_id, '189: What is the classic appearance of blood in an infant with severe methemoglobinemia?', '["Bright cherry red", "Chocolate brown", "Pale pink", "Black"]', 1, 'Methemoglobin cannot carry oxygen, leading to cyanosis that does not improve with oxygen, and blood that appears dark brown.'),
    (v_topic_id, '190: What is the antidote for methemoglobinemia?', '["N-acetylcysteine", "Oxygen", "Methylene blue", "Atropine"]', 2, 'Methylene blue acts as an electron donor to reduce methemoglobin back to hemoglobin.'),
    (v_topic_id, '191: A child ingests a large amount of their mother''s prenatal vitamins containing iron. They present with hematemesis and shock. What is the antidote?', '["Dimercaprol", "Deferoxamine", "Penicillamine", "Activated charcoal"]', 1, 'Deferoxamine is an iron chelator. It causes the urine to turn a "vin rose" (pink-red) color as iron is excreted.'),
    (v_topic_id, '192: A teenager presents with Kayser-Fleischer rings (brown rings in the cornea), resting tremor, and elevated liver enzymes. What is the diagnosis?', '["Hemochromatosis", "Wilson disease", "Menkes disease", "Cystic fibrosis"]', 1, 'Wilson disease is a defect in copper excretion (ATP7B gene), leading to copper accumulation in the liver, brain (basal ganglia), and eyes.'),
    (v_topic_id, '193: What is the treatment for Wilson disease?', '["Iron chelation", "Copper chelation (e.g., Penicillamine or Trientine) and oral Zinc", "Vitamin B12", "Liver transplant only"]', 1, 'Chelators remove excess copper, while Zinc blocks intestinal copper absorption.'),
    (v_topic_id, '194: A child with a genetic defect in the HFE gene has excessive iron absorption, leading to bronze skin, diabetes, and cirrhosis. Diagnosis?', '["Wilson disease", "Hemochromatosis", "Thalassemia", "Sideroblastic anemia"]', 1, 'Primary hemochromatosis causes massive iron overload ("bronze diabetes"). Treatment is therapeutic phlebotomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, '195: A breastfed infant is diagnosed with galactosemia. What long-term complication remains a significant risk despite strict dietary control?', '["Liver failure", "Cataracts", "Primary ovarian insufficiency (premature menopause) in females", "Heart failure"]', 2, 'Even with a galactose-free diet, endogenous galactose production can cause toxic metabolites that damage the ovaries.'),
    (v_topic_id, '196: A child presents with pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia). They have a history of a carcinoid tumor. Why does carcinoid syndrome cause pellagra?', '["The tumor secretes niacin", "The tumor consumes large amounts of tryptophan to make serotonin, depleting niacin precursors", "The tumor destroys the gut lining", "The tumor causes malabsorption"]', 1, 'Tryptophan is diverted away from niacin synthesis to produce massive amounts of serotonin.'),
    (v_topic_id, '197: A patient with severe Anorexia Nervosa is found to have osteopenia on a DEXA scan. What is the primary cause?', '["Lack of dietary calcium", "Estrogen deficiency (hypothalamic amenorrhea) combined with elevated cortisol", "Vitamin D deficiency", "Lack of weight-bearing exercise"]', 1, 'Estrogen is crucial for bone density. The starvation state causes a massive hormonal shift that rapidly depletes bone.'),
    (v_topic_id, '198: A toddler with a diet consisting almost entirely of highly processed snack foods and no vegetables presents with severe constipation. What dietary modification is most important?', '["Increase protein", "Increase dietary fiber and water intake", "Decrease fat", "Add a multivitamin"]', 1, 'Lack of dietary fiber is the leading cause of functional constipation in toddlers.'),
    (v_topic_id, '199: A child with Failure to Thrive is observed during feeding. The mother is anxious, the child avoids eye contact, and meals are a battleground. What is the most likely etiology?', '["Celiac disease", "Psychosocial (Non-organic) Failure to Thrive", "Cystic fibrosis", "Food allergy"]', 1, 'Disturbed parent-child interaction is a hallmark of psychosocial FTT.'),
    (v_topic_id, '200: Final Pearl: In any child presenting with Failure to Thrive, what non-invasive plot must be examined before any blood is drawn?', '["The child''s sleep chart", "The child''s growth chart (Weight, Height, Head Circumference over time)", "The parent''s weight", "The sibling''s height"]', 1, 'The trajectory of the growth curves provides the most critical diagnostic information (e.g., distinguishing familial short stature from true malnutrition).');
